```c
#define HALF_PI 1.57079632679f
#define TAU_F   6.28318530718f

static void sincosf(float a, float *os, float *oc) {
    float q = a * (1.0f / TAU_F);
    float t = (q - (float)(int)q) * TAU_F;   /* [0, 2pi) */
    float u;
    u32 quad;
    if (t < HALF_PI)            { quad = 0; u = t; }
    else if (t < TAU_F - HALF_PI){ quad = 1; u = TAU_F - t; }
    else if (t < HALF_PI + PI)   { quad = 2; u = t - PI; }
    else                        { quad = 3; u = TAU_F - t; }
    float u2 = u * u;
    float s = u * (1.0f - u2 * (1.0f/6.0f - u2 * (1.0f/120.0f)));
    float c = 1.0f + u2 * (-0.5f + u2 * (1.0f/24.0f - u2*(1.0f/720.0f)));
    switch (quad) {
      case 1: *os = s; *oc = -c; break;
      case 2: *os = -s; *oc = -c; break;
      case 3: *os = -s; *oc = c; break;
      default: *os = s; *oc = c; break;
    }
}
```
Check quadrant mapping with t in [0,2π):
- t ∈ [0, π/2): quad 0, u = t → (sin, cos) = (s, c). ✓
- t ∈ [π/2, π): u = 2π - t ∈ (π, 3π/2]... hmm that's wrong. sin(2π - t) = -sin(t). For t ∈ [π/2, π], we want sin t ≥ 0. Let me redo: quadrant 1 = t ∈ [π/2, π): sin = sin(π - t), cos = -cos(π - t). So u = π - t ∈ (0, π/2], s = sin(u) = sin(t), c = cos(u) = -cos(t) ✓. So quad 1: u = PI - t, (s, -c). Fix.
- quadrant 2: t ∈ [π, 3π/2): u = t - π ∈ [0, π/2); sin t = -sin u, cos t = -cos u → (-s, -c). ✓
- quadrant 3: t ∈ [3π/2, 2π): u = 2π - t ∈ (0, π/2]; sin t = -sin u, cos t = cos u → (-s, c). ✓

So:
```
if (t < HALF_PI) { quad=0; u=t; }
else if (t < PI) { quad=1; u=PI-t; }
else if (t < PI+HALF_PI) { quad=2; u=t-PI; }
else { quad=3; u=TAU-t; }
```
